Language Models with Language Models.‚Äù","‚ÄúRed Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms: Methods, Scaling Behaviors, and Lessons Learned.‚Äù","‚ÄúFLIRT: Feedback Loop In-context Red Teaming.‚Äù","‚ÄúExplore, Establish, Exploit: Red Teaming Language Models from Scratch.‚Äù","‚ÄúDefending ChatGPT against Jailbreak Attack via Self-Reminder.‚Äù","‚ÄúAutomatically Auditing Large Language Models via Discrete Optimization.‚Äù","‚ÄúCompromising Real-World LLM-Integrated Applications with Indirect Prompt Injection.‚Äù","‚ÄúBaseline Defenses for Adversarial Attacks Against Aligned Language Models.‚Äù","‚ÄúJailbroken: How Does LLM Safety Training Fail?‚Äù","‚ÄúEDA: Easy data augmentation techniques for boosting performance on text classification tasks.‚Äù","www.jailbreakchat.com","‚ÄúYou can use GPT-4 to create prompt injections against GPT-4‚Äù","Nlp","Language-Model","Safety","Adversarial Attacks","Robustness","Redteam"],"languages":["eng"],"chroma:document":"Then $B < kL$ token substitution candidates ${\\mathbf{t}^{(1)}, \\dots, \\mathbf{t}^{(B)}}$ are selected out of $kL$ options at random and the one with best loss (i.e. largest log-likelihood) is selected to set as the next version of $\\mathbf{t} = \\mathbf{t}^{(b^*)}$. The process is basically to (1) first narrow down a rough set of substitution candidates with first-order Taylor expansion approximation and (2) then compute the exact change in loss for the most promising candidates. Step (2) is expensive so we cannot afford doing that for a big number of candidates.","category":"CompositeElement","element_id":"2775507ceeaaa257529304164be2b816"}␀␀␀␀o␀n␀g␀
