dataset","Ziegler et al. (2022)","Wallace et al. (2019)","BERT-Attack","Xu et al. 2021","BAD dataset","Tensorflow dataset","red-teaming dataset","Ganguli, et al. 2022","GPT-4","DALL-E 3","#","Perez et al. 2022","A2C","BAD (Bot Adversarial Dialogue) dataset","nucleus sampling","Casper et al. (2023)","Perez et al. (2022)","here","here","here","Mehrabi et al. 2023","in-context learning","Perez et al. 2022","Schramowski et al. 2022","https://github.com/notAI-tech/NudeNet)","Perspective API","#","#","Madry et al. 2017","#","Xie et al. 2023","Jain et al. 2023","White-box attacks","UAT-LM","Jain et al. 2023","#","#","‚ÄúTowards Deep Learning Models Resistant to Adversarial Attacks‚Äù","‚ÄúSemantically equivalent adversarial rules for debugging NLP models‚Äù","‚ÄúGradient-based adversarial attacks against text transformers‚Äù","‚ÄúHotFlip: White-Box Adversarial Examples for Text Classification‚Äù","‚ÄúUniversal Adversarial Triggers for Attacking and Analyzing NLP.‚Äù","code","‚ÄúRobust Conversational Agents against Imperceptible Toxicity Triggers.‚Äù","‚ÄúUniversal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned Language Models.‚Äù","‚ÄúRLPrompt: Optimizing Discrete Text Prompts with Reinforcement Learning.‚Äù","‚ÄúIs BERT Really Robust? A Strong Baseline for Natural Language Attack on Text Classification and Entailment.‚Äù","‚ÄúBERT-Attack: Adversarial Attack Against BERT Using BERT.‚Äù","\"TextAttack: A Framework for Adversarial Attacks, Data Augmentation, and Adversarial Training in NLP.\"","‚ÄúBot-Adversarial Dialogue for Safe Conversational Agents.‚Äù","‚ÄúAdversarial training for high-stakes reliability.‚Äù","‚ÄúRed Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms: Methods, Scaling Behaviors, and Lessons Learned.‚Äù","‚ÄúRed Teaming Language Models with Language Models.‚Äù","‚ÄúRed Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms: Methods, Scaling Behaviors, and Lessons Learned.‚Äù","‚ÄúFLIRT: Feedback Loop In-context Red Teaming.‚Äù","‚ÄúExplore, Establish, Exploit: Red Teaming Language Models from Scratch.‚Äù","‚ÄúDefending ChatGPT against Jailbreak Attack via Self-Reminder.‚Äù","‚ÄúAutomatically Auditing Large Language Models via Discrete Optimization.‚Äù","‚ÄúCompromising Real-World LLM-Integrated Applications with Indirect Prompt Injection.‚Äù","‚ÄúBaseline Defenses for Adversarial Attacks Against Aligned Language Models.‚Äù","‚ÄúJailbroken: How Does LLM Safety Training Fail?‚Äù","‚ÄúEDA: Easy data augmentation techniques for boosting performance on text classification tasks.‚Äù","www.jailbreakchat.com","‚ÄúYou can use GPT-4 to create prompt injections against GPT-4‚Äù","Nlp","Language-Model","Safety","Adversarial Attacks","Robustness","Redteam"],"chroma:document":"Only when the current $\\mathbf{t}$ successfully triggers ${ (\\mathbf{x}^{(i)}, \\mathbf{y}^{(i)}) }_{i=1}^{m_c}$, we increase $m_c = m_c + 1$. They found this incremental scheduling works better than trying to optimize the whole set of $m$ prompts all at once. This approximates to curriculum learning.\n\nThe above step 1-3 are repeated for a number of iterations.\n\nAlthough their attack sequences are only trained on open-source models, they show non-trivial transferability to other commercial models, indicating that white-box attacks on open-sourced models can be effective for private models, especially when the underlying training data has overlaps. Note that Vicuna is trained with data collected from GPT-3.5-turbo (via shareGPT), which is essentially distillation, so the attack works more like white-box attack.","languages":["eng"],"url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-10-25-adv-attack-llm/","link_urls":["#token-manipulation","https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.05909","https://www.aclweb.org/anthology/P18-1079/","https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2022-04-15-data-gen/#EDA","https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.11196","https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.11932","https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.500.pdf","#gradient-based-attacks","https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13733","https://arxiv.org/abs/