weng.github.io/tags/adversarial-attacks/","https://lilianweng.github.io/tags/robustness/","https://lilianweng.github.io/tags/redteam/"],"languages":["eng"],"emphasized_text_tags":["b","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","b","i","i","i","b","b","b","b","b","b","i","b","b","i","b","b","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","b","i","b","b","i","b","i","i","i","b","b","i","i","i","i","i","b","i","i"],"emphasized_text_contents":["black box","What","NOUN","‚ÜíWhich","NOUN","WP","is ‚Üí","WP","‚Äôs‚Äô","was‚Üíis","GBDA","make adversarial loss optimization differentiable","extreme","‚Äúa similarity score for evaluating text generation that captures the semantic similarity between pairwise tokens in contextualized embeddings of a transformer model.‚Äù","HotFlip","Universal Adversarial Triggers","UAT","AutoPrompt","UAT-LM","UTSC","unigram","affirmative","greedy coordinate gradient (GCG)","transferability","ARCA","coordinate ascent","should have been mitigated","Competing objective","Mismatched generalization","Display of saliency score of each token","Token substitution and insertion","Zero-shot generation","Stochastic few-shot generation","Supervised learning","Reinforcement learning","Explore","Establish","Exploit","FLIRT","update in-context exemplars","FIFO","LIFO","the last one","Scoring","effectiveness","diversity","low-toxicity","Scoring-LIFO","robust","inner maximization","outer minimization","Inner maximization","Outer minimization","instruct","adversarial training","Paraphrase","Retokenization"],"category":"CompositeElement","link_texts":["#","Morris et al. 2020","Ribeiro et al (2018)","EDA","Wei & Zou 2019","Jin et al. 2019","Li et al. 2020","#","Guo et al. 2021","Jang et al. 2016","Zhang et al. 2019","Ebrahimi et al. 2018","Wallace et al. (2019)","HotFlip","Shin et al., 2020","Mehrabi et al. (2022)","Zou et al. (2023)","OpenAI 2023","UAT and AutoPrompt","Jones et al. 2023","#","Wei et al. (2023)","DAN","AIM","it can understand","Wei et al. (2023)","Greshake et al. (2023)","#","Wallace et al. (2019)","QuizBowl QA dataset","Ziegler et al. (2022)","Wallace et al. (2019)","BERT-Attack","Xu et al. 2021","BAD dataset","Tensorflow dataset","red-teaming dataset","Ganguli, et al. 2022","GPT-4","DALL-E 3","#","Perez et al. 2022","A2C","BAD (Bot Adversarial Dialogue) dataset","nucleus sampling","Casper et al. (2023)","Perez et al. (2022)","here","here","here","Mehrabi et al. 2023","in-context learning","Perez et al. 2022","Schramowski et al. 2022","https://github.com/notAI-tech/NudeNet)","Perspective API","#","#","Madry et al. 2017","#","Xie et al. 2023","Jain et al. 2023","White-box attacks","UAT-LM","Jain et al. 2023","#","#","‚ÄúTowards Deep Learning Models Resistant to Adversarial Attacks‚Äù","‚ÄúSemantically equivalent adversarial rules for debugging NLP models‚Äù","‚ÄúGradient-based adversarial attacks against text transformers‚Äù","‚ÄúHotFlip: White-Box Adversarial Examples for Text Classification‚Äù","‚ÄúUniversal Adversarial Triggers for Attacking and Analyzing NLP.‚Äù","code","‚ÄúRobust Conversational Agents against Imperceptible Toxicity Triggers.‚Äù","‚ÄúUniversal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned Language Models.‚Äù","‚ÄúRLPrompt: Optimizing Discrete Text Prompts with Reinforcement Learning.‚Äù","‚ÄúIs BERT Really Robust? A Strong Baseline for Natural Language Attack on Text Classification and Entailment.‚Äù","‚ÄúBERT-Attack: Adversarial Attack Against BERT Using BERT.‚Äù","\"TextAttack: A Framework for Adversarial Attacks, Data Augmentation, and Adversarial Training in NLP.\"","‚ÄúBot-Adversarial Dialogue for Safe Conversational Agents.‚Äù","‚ÄúAdversarial training for high-stakes reliability.‚Äù","‚ÄúRed Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms: Methods, Scaling Behaviors, and Lessons Learned.‚Äù","‚ÄúRed Teaming Language Models with Language Models.‚Äù","‚ÄúRed Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms: Methods, Scaling Behaviors, and Lessons Learned.‚Äù","‚ÄúFLIRT: Feedback Loop In-context Red Teaming.‚Äù","‚ÄúExplore, Establish, Exploit: Red Teaming Language Models from Scratch.‚Äù","‚ÄúDefending ChatGPT against Jailbreak Attack via Self-Reminder.‚Äù","‚ÄúAutomatically Auditing Large Language Models via Discrete Optimization.‚Äù","‚ÄúCompromising Real-World LLM-Integrated Applications with Indirect Prompt Injection.‚Äù","‚ÄúBaseline Defenses for Adversarial Attacks Against Aligned Language Models.‚Äù","‚ÄúJailbroken: How Does LLM Safety Training Fail?‚Äù","‚ÄúEDA: Easy data augmentation techniques for boosting performance on text classification tasks.‚Äù","www.jailbreakchat.com","‚ÄúYou can use GPT-4 to create prompt injections against GPT-4‚Äù","Nlp","Language-Model","Safety","Adversarial Attacks","Robustness","Redteam"],"url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-10-25-adv-attack-llm/","filetype":"text/html","chroma:document":"However, the gradient of LLM log-likelihood w.r.t. the $i$-th token embedding $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{e}_{y_i}} \\log p(\\mathbf{y}_{1:i}\\mid \\mathbf{x})$ is ill-formed, because the output prediction of $p(\\mathbf{y}_{1:i}\\mid \\mathbf{x})$ is a probability distribution over the token vocabulary space where no token embedding is involved and thus the gradient is 0. To resolve this, ARCA decomposes the score $s_i$ into two terms, a linearly approximatable term $s_i^\\text{lin}$ and an autoregressive term $s^\\text{aut}_i$, and only applies approximation on the $s_i^\\text{lin} \\to \\tilde{s}_i^\\text{lin}$:","element_id":"2775507ceeaaa257529304164be2b816","orig_elements":""}␀␀␀","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-06-23-agent/","emphasized_text_contents":["Planning","Memory","Tool use","Chain of thought","Tree of Thoughts","LLM+P","ReAct","Reflexion","decide to reset","Chain of Hindsight","Algorithm Distillation","algorithm","Sensory Memory","Short-Term Memory","Working Memory","Long-Term Memory","approximate nearest neighbors (ANN)‚Äã","LSH","hashing","ANNOY","random projection trees","HNSW","FAISS","clustering","ScaNN","anisotropic vector quantization","MRKL","knowing when to and how to use the tools are crucial","TALM","Toolformer","HuggingGPT","(1) Task planning","(2) Model selection","(3) Task execution","(4) Response generation","API-Bank","call the API","retrieve the API","plan API beyond retrieve and call","ChemCrow","Generative Agents","Memory","observation","Retrieval","Reflection","higher-level summaries of past events","Planning & Reacting","Finite context length","Challenges in long-term planning and task decomposition","Reliability of natural language interface"],"chroma:document":"The LLM is provided with a list of tool names, descriptions of their utility, and details about the expected input/output.\n\nIt is then instructed to answer a user-given prompt using the tools provided when necessary. The instruction suggests the model to follow the ReAct format - Thought, Action, Action Input, Observation.\n\nOne interesting observation is that while the LLM-based evaluation concluded that GPT-4 and ChemCrow perform nearly equivalently, human evaluations with experts oriented towards the completion and chemical correctness of the solutions showed that ChemCrow outperforms GPT-4 by a large margin. This indicates a potential problem with using LLM to evaluate its own performance on domains that requires deep expertise. The lack of expertise may cause LLMs not knowing its flaws and thus cannot well judge the correctness of task results.\n\nBoiko et al. (2023) also looked into LLM-empowered agents for scientific discovery, to handle autonomous design, planning, and performance of complex scientific experiments. This agent can use tools to browse the Internet, read documentation, execute code, call robotics experimentation APIs and leverage other LLMs.","emphasized_text_tags":["b","b","b","b","b","b","b{"chroma:document":"$$ \\begin{aligned} s_i(\\mathbf{v}; \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) &= s^\\text{lin}_i(\\mathbf{v}; \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) + s^\\text{aut}_i(\\mathbf{v}; \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) \\\\ s^\\text{lin}_i(\\mathbf{v}; \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) &= \\phi(\\mathbf{x}, [\\mathbf{y}_{1:i-1}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{y}_{i+1:n}]) + \\lambda_\\text{LLM}\\;p( \\mathbf{y}_{i+1:n} \\mid \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}_{1:i-1}, \\mathbf{v}) \\\\ \\tilde{s}^\\text{lin}_i(\\mathbf{v}; \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) &=","link_texts":["#","Morris et al. 2020","Ribeiro et al (2018)","EDA","Wei & Zou 2019","Jin et al. 2019","Li et al. 2020","#","Guo et al. 2021","Jang et al. 2016","Zhang et al. 2019","Ebrahimi et al. 2018","Wallace et al. (2019)","HotFlip","Shin et al., 2020","Mehrabi et al. (2022)","Zou et al. (2023)","OpenAI 2023","UAT and AutoPrompt","Jones et al. 2023","#","Wei et al. (2023)","DAN","AIM","it can understand","Wei et al. (2023)","Greshake et al. (2023)","#","Wallace et al. (2019)","QuizBowl QA dataset","Ziegler et al. (2022)","Wallace et al. (2019)","BERT-Attack","Xu et al. 2021","BAD dataset","Tensorflow dataset","red-teaming dataset","Ganguli, et al. 2022","GPT-4","DALL-E 3","#","Perez et al. 2022","A2C","BAD (Bot Adversarial Dialogue) dataset","nucleus sampling","Casper et al. (2023)","Perez et al. (2022)","here","here","here","Mehrabi et al. 2023","in-context learning","Perez et al. 2022","Schramowski et al. 2022","https://github.com/notAI-tech/NudeNet)","Perspective API","#","#","Madry et al. 2017","#","Xie et al. 2023","Jain et al. 2023","White-box attacks","UAT-LM","Jain et al. 2023","#","#","‚ÄúTowards Deep Learning Models Resistant to Adversarial Attacks‚Äù","‚ÄúSemantically equivalent adversarial rules for debugging NLP models‚Äù","‚ÄúGradient-based adversarial attacks against text transformers‚Äù","‚ÄúHotFlip: White-Box Adversarial Examples for Text Classification‚Äù","‚ÄúUniversal Adversarial Triggers for Attacking and Analyzing NLP.‚Äù","code","‚ÄúRobust Conversational Agents against Imperceptible Toxicity Triggers.‚Äù","‚ÄúUniversal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned Language Models.‚Äù","‚ÄúRLPrompt: Optimizing D